---
title: "How Close Can a Before/After Comparison Get to the Truth?"
subtitle: "A Synthetic Panel with Known Ground Truth"
draft: true
draft-mode: unlinked
date: "2026-10-06"
categories: ["causal-inference", "statistics"]
---





Suppose you are a data scientist at an AVOD service.

On 2026-06-01, users became able to pause a video ad, but only on iOS devices.
Users cannot skip ads while watching. So, before this feature, the only way to
stop watching during an ad was to close the app or the browser. In that case,
the play was counted as incomplete. This feature is expected to replace some of
these exits, so the completion rate on iOS should increase.

After the release, the dashboard shows a higher completion rate on iOS than
before. You'd want to report "the feature is working," but all you have is daily
completion rates by device. Data in this format, the same units (devices, in
this case) observed repeatedly over time, is called a panel, and I'll use that
term from here on. So, can we look at this panel and say "the feature lifted the
completion rate by X points"?

This article does two things:

1. Before/after comparison: compute the estimate everyone tries first, and
   confirm with numbers that it cannot separate an effect from everything else
   moving at the same time. As we'll see, running it on a date where nothing
   was released produces an "effect" of $+0.62$ percentage points (pp from here
   on), around 60% of the true effect, and nothing in the data marks it as
   spurious.

2. Synthetic data design: build the validation dataset used throughout this
   series. The series covers difference-in-differences (DiD), the synthetic
   control method (SCM), CausalImpact, and a randomized comparison, and the
   backbone of all four is synthetic data whose true treatment effect is known.
   As long as we know the answer, we can score how far off each method is.

## Defining the metrics

An impression is one ad play start, and a complete is a play that ran to the
end. The completion rate is defined as
$\text{complete} / \text{impression}$. The table below maps how a play can end
to the two metrics; every play that starts counts as exactly one impression,
however it ends.

  | How the play ends                                | impression | complete |
  | ------------------------------------------------ | ---------: | -------: |
  | Played to the end                                |          1 |        1 |
  | Paused, then resumed and played to the end       |          1 |        1 |
  | App or browser closed to stop watching (an exit) |          1 |        0 |
  | Cut off by a network failure or an app crash     |          1 |        0 |

The pause feature can only act on the third row. The mechanism from the opening
is exactly this: shift some of those exits into the second row, and the
completion rate goes up. It cannot rescue unintentional incomplete plays like
the fourth row. This boundary matters later, in the sanity check on the size of
the true effect.

One more assumption: whether a play counts as complete is settled within the
same day. Pauses are assumed to be short, so a play that starts on day $t$ is
resolved as complete or incomplete within day $t$.

## A before/after comparison on iOS alone

The simplest way to measure the effect is to compare the completion rates before
and after the release. Pick an anchor date $t_0$ and time window lengths
$k_{\text{pre}}, k_{\text{post}}$, then compute the following difference:

$$
  \hat{\Delta}(t_0;\, k_{\text{pre}}, k_{\text{post}}) = \frac{\sum_{t \in W_{\text{post}}} x_t}{\sum_{t \in W_{\text{post}}} n_t} - \frac{\sum_{t \in W_{\text{pre}}} x_t}{\sum_{t \in W_{\text{pre}}} n_t}
$$

Here, $n_t$ and $x_t$ are the impressions and completes for iOS on day $t$, with
the time windows defined as $W_{\text{pre}} = [t_0 - k_{\text{pre}},\, t_0)$ and
$W_{\text{post}} = [t_0,\, t_0 + k_{\text{post}})$. Results are reported in pp.

For comparison, prepare a fake anchor date as well: run the same calculation on
a date with no release anywhere near it, and watch how the estimator behaves
where the true answer is zero. This is the same idea as a placebo in drug
trials, hence the name: the placebo anchor date. The date is 2026-03-02, a
Monday, matching the day of the week of the actual release. It also splits the
pre-period in half, so a 91-day window on each side of it covers the pre-period
exactly.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Daily completion rate on iOS. The dashed line marks the feature release date (2026-06-01), the dotted line the placebo anchor date (2026-03-02).](index_files/figure-html/fig-ios-series-1.png){#fig-ios-series width=672}
:::
:::


@fig-ios-series shows three movements mixed together in the series: a weekly
cycle that dips 0.4 to 0.6pp on weekends, a slow trend that drifts down by about
0.6pp over the eight months, and daily noise with a standard deviation of around
0.4pp. A before/after difference picks up all three, with no way to tell them
apart from the treatment effect.

@tbl-naive-grid shows how much gets picked up: four time windows anchored at the
release date, and four more at the placebo anchor date 13 weeks earlier, eight
estimates in total. The three shorter windows are the same on both sides. The
longest one differs, because each anchor has a different amount of data around
it: 182 days before and 61 days after the release, and 91 days on each side of
the placebo anchor.




::: {#tbl-naive-grid .cell tbl-cap='A before/after comparison on iOS across anchor dates and time window lengths.'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

