@fig-panel-devices shows requirements 2 and 3 realized. All 11 series share the
same weekend dip and the same downward trend; what differs is the level and the
coarseness of the noise, which follows how small $M_d$ is. The iOS pre-period
level is 93.1%, fourth lowest of the 11 devices, sitting neatly inside the
control range of 91.5 to 97.1%.

### The true treatment effect

The true value used here is the average treatment effect on the treated (ATT),
on the pp scale. Concretely, for each iOS post-period cell, take the difference
between the generating rate with $\tau$ and the generating rate without it, then
average those differences weighted by impressions.

$$
  \text{ATT} = \frac{\sum_{t \geq T} n_{\text{ios},t} \left[ \sigma(\ell_{\text{ios},t}) - \sigma(\ell_{\text{ios},t} - \tau) \right]}{\sum_{t \geq T} n_{\text{ios},t}} = +1.049 \text{ pp}
$$

$\sigma$ is the logistic function and $\ell$ the linear predictor including the
treatment. This value comes from the generating equation, not from an estimate
based on the realized completes. The only part the seed touches is the weight
$n$ drawn from the Poisson, so it works as a fixed yardstick in practice. From
here on, every estimator in this series is reported next to this $+1.049$pp.

The size of the effect deserves a sanity check against the mechanism. Without
treatment, the iOS completion rate on a weekday at the start of the period is
$\sigma(2.67) = 93.5\%$, which means 6.5pp of plays end incomplete. A true
effect of $+1.05$pp converts one sixth of that into completes. Incomplete plays
also include network failures and crashes, so among deliberate exits alone, the
share the pause feature takes over is larger than one sixth. That is an
aggressive number for a single feature, but the order of magnitude is realistic,
and it is nowhere near a fantasy value that would eat up the entire pool of
incomplete plays.

### Two variants from one random stream

The generator outputs two datasets: parallel, the main line, and divergent, in
which iOS alone drifts away from the pre-period onward. The divergent variant
adds $\delta \cdot t / T_{\text{total}}$ ($\delta = +0.30$) to the iOS logit,
opening a gap of about 1.3pp by the end of the pre-period. In the second
article, this variant plays the world where the assumption behind the method
breaks.

The trick is in `qbinom`. `qbinom(r, n, p)` passes a uniform draw $r$ through
the binomial quantile function; the distribution is the same as
`rbinom(1, n, p)`, but the result is determined once $r$ is fixed. A single $r$
per cell is shared by both variants, so control cells, where both $n$ and $p$
are identical, produce bit-identical completes (verified:
`all(complete_parallel == complete_divergent)` returns `TRUE` for the control
devices). The iOS cells have different $p$ across variants, so their values do
not match, but they pass through the same $r$ and their noise moves in the same
direction (comonotone). If an analysis of the two files differs, then, the
difference comes from the divergent trend term, not from a fresh draw of the
random numbers.

The output files are `panel_parallel.csv` and `panel_divergent.csv`, both with
the schema `date, device, post, impression, complete, complete_rate`. `post` can
be derived from the release date, but it is kept as a column for convenience.

## Decomposing the comparison with the true values

Requirement 1 pays off right here. With synthetic data, both the treated
generating rate $p_{dt} = \sigma(\ell_{dt})$ and the untreated one
$p^0_{dt} = \sigma(\ell_{dt} - \tau \cdot 1\{d = \text{ios},\ t \geq T\})$ are
available for every cell. Replacing the completes inside $\hat{\Delta}$ with
these rates gives the expectation conditional on the impression counts, and that
expectation splits exactly into a treatment component and a contamination
component.

$$
  \hat{\Delta} = \underbrace{\left[ m(W_{\text{post}}) - m(W_{\text{pre}}) \right] - \left[ m^0(W_{\text{post}}) - m^0(W_{\text{pre}}) \right]}_{\text{treatment}} + \underbrace{m^0(W_{\text{post}}) - m^0(W_{\text{pre}})}_{\text{contamination}} + \text{noise}
$$

Here $m(W) = \sum_{t \in W} n_t p_t / \sum_{t \in W} n_t$, and $m^0$ is the same
quantity computed with $p^0$. The contamination term is the before/after
difference in a world where $\tau = 0$; the influence of the trend and of the
day-of-week composition of the window collects here. The noise is the residual,
the realized value minus its expectation.


