@tbl-decomp is the reveal behind @tbl-naive-grid. The treatment component barely
moves across the four release-side windows, staying between $+1.02$ and
$+1.05$pp; what looked like movement was contamination and noise all along.

Contamination changes sign with the window. For the longest release-side window
it is $-0.31$pp, and the cause is the trend: the centers of the pre-period and
the post-period are 0.50 apart in elapsed fraction $t/T_{\text{total}}$, about
four months in days, and a logit decline of $-0.10 \times 0.50 = -0.050$ works
out to $-0.31$pp at the iOS level. For the 3-day window it is $+0.29$pp, and the
cause is the day-of-week composition: the pre side is Friday through Sunday
(average $w$ of $-0.043$) and the post side is Monday through Wednesday
($w = 0$), so that gap carries straight through. The 7-day and 28-day windows
are balanced across days of the week and too short for the trend to bite, so
contamination is nearly zero; noise of $+0.31$ to $+0.38$pp takes over as the
main source of error instead.

In real data, only the first numeric column of this table is observable. On a
real panel there is no way to split the $+1.41$pp from the 7-day window into
"$+1.04$ of effect and $+0.38$ of luck." That single point is the reason this
series runs on synthetic data. In the articles that follow, each estimate is
reported next to the true value of $+1.049$pp, and the gap between them is
settled by attribution rather than argument.

## R code

The generator below is the canonical implementation for this series. Running it
writes the two CSV files that every later article reads, so the next article on
DiD starts from `data/panel_parallel.csv` rather than regenerating the panel.

``` r
library(tidyverse)

devices <- tribble(
  ~device           , ~treated , ~alpha , ~m_users ,
  "ios"             , TRUE     , 2.67   ,     2700 ,
  "android"         , FALSE    , 2.51   ,     1300 ,
  "pc-web"          , FALSE    , 2.51   ,      800 ,
  "android-tv"      , FALSE    , 3.32   ,      800 ,
  "smart-tv-a"      , FALSE    , 3.06   ,      800 ,
  "smart-tv-b"      , FALSE    , 2.44   ,      800 ,
  "smart-tv-c"      , FALSE    , 3.48   ,      500 ,
  "tv-web"          , FALSE    , 3.18   ,      500 ,
  "mobile-web"      , FALSE    , 2.84   ,      500 ,
  "streaming-stick" , FALSE    , 3.55   ,      300 ,
  "game-console"    , FALSE    , 2.94   ,      300
)

w_dow <- tribble(
  ~wday , ~w    ,
      1 ,  0    , # Mon
      2 ,  0    , # Tue
      3 ,  0    , # Wed
      4 ,  0    , # Thu
      5 ,  0.02 , # Fri
      6 , -0.06 , # Sat
      7 , -0.09 # Sun
)

# logit-scale components (gamma, tau, delta in the article notation)
trend_common <- -0.10 # common trend over the full period
effect_treatment <- 0.17 # treatment effect on ios
trend_divergence <- 0.30 # ios-specific trend, divergent variant only
imp_per_user_day <- 1.5 # expected impressions per user-day
date_treat <- lubridate::ymd("2026-06-01")

days <- tibble(
  date = seq(
    lubridate::ymd("2025-12-01"),
    lubridate::ymd("2026-07-31"),
    by = "day"
  )
) |>
  mutate(
    wday = lubridate::wday(date, week_start = 1),
    post = date >= date_treat,
    t_frac = (row_number() - 1) / (n() - 1)
  ) |>
  inner_join(w_dow, by = "wday") |>
  arrange(date)

# inverse transform sampling: qbinom(r, n, p) is deterministic given r,
# so both variants share randomness and control units coincide exactly

set.seed(1)

panel_gen <- crossing(devices, days) |>
  mutate(
    impression = rpois(n(), imp_per_user_day * m_users),
    r = runif(n()),
    logit_parallel = alpha +
      w +
      trend_common * t_frac +
      effect_treatment * post * treated,
    logit_divergent = logit_parallel + trend_divergence * t_frac * treated,
    complete_parallel = qbinom(r, impression, plogis(logit_parallel)),
    complete_divergent = qbinom(r, impression, plogis(logit_divergent))
  )

panel <- panel_gen |>
  pivot_longer(
    c(complete_parallel, complete_divergent),
    names_to = "variant",
    names_prefix = "complete_",
    values_to = "complete"
  ) |>
  mutate(complete_rate = complete / impression) |>
  select(variant, date, device, post, impression, complete, complete_rate)

# ATT: derived from the generating equation, not estimated from the completes
panel_gen |>
  filter(treated, post) |>
  summarise(
    att = weighted.mean(
      plogis(logit_parallel) - plogis(logit_parallel - effect_treatment),
      impression
    )
  )
#> # A tibble: 1 x 1
#>      att
#>    <dbl>
#> 1 0.0105

# structural check: control units identical across variants, by construction
panel_gen |>
  filter(!treated) |>
  summarise(identical = all(complete_parallel == complete_divergent))
#> # A tibble: 1 x 1
#>   identical
#>   <lgl>
#> 1 TRUE

dir.create("data", showWarnings = FALSE)

panel |>
  filter(variant == "parallel") |>
  select(-variant) |>
  write_csv("data/panel_parallel.csv")

panel |>
  filter(variant == "divergent") |>
  select(-variant) |>
  write_csv("data/panel_divergent.csv")
```

Each file has 2,673 rows, 11 devices over 243 days, with the schema
`date, device, post, impression, complete, complete_rate`. The `variant` column
is dropped on the way out, so the two files are interchangeable as inputs: an
analysis written against one runs unchanged against the other.
